THE BOTTOM EDGE OF THE UNMAKING
A Chronicle of the Obsidian Lanterns

PART I: THE FRIGHTENED TOWN
The map came out on the cart bed once more, and this time it was Ivarstead they found on it — a huddle of roofs pinned into the bottom corner of a mountain too big for the ground it stood on. They traced the road from where they stood to where they needed to be, and above the town, filling the whole top of the map and then some, the Throat of the World climbed out of the page. Seven thousand steps lay between here and there. Someone said it would be one of the tallest places in Skyrim, and meant it as a joke about the climb ahead, and was more right than the joke intended.

Smoke was still rising from somewhere in the town. That meant someone was still down there to light a fire.

The road crossed a stone bridge and put them inside Ivarstead proper — five or six houses crowded onto ground that had no business being flat this close to a mountain that size. Half the doors stood open. Nothing moved. No one came out to meet them.

They searched the houses together, all but one of them. Ismara peeled off on her own to look through a house further down the row, and didn't wait for anyone to agree it was a good idea. The rest stayed as a group. The first house they entered was a meal caught mid-interruption — a table set for four, chairs shoved back at every angle, a pot burned to char over a dead hearth. The door bar hadn't been lifted; it had been snapped clean in half, and lay in two pieces on the floor. Someone crouched to read the wood grain of the break and found drag-gouges scored into the floor, one direction only, from the back window to the broken front door. Whatever had come in through that window hadn't needed to force its own way back out. It had already been leaving, and dragging something with it.

The second house was a child's room. The bed was made, corners tucked, the kind of made that only happens before anything has gone wrong. The window shutters were torn off their hinges and thrown outward, and three claw marks raked the doorframe on the way through — too high for a child, too low for a grown man's shoulder. Whatever had gone out that window had a body shaped like neither.

The third was the mill owner's house, tools hung in a neat row, a half-carved wooden toy still sitting in its own sawdust on the bench, the knife stopped mid-cut and never picked back up. Under a stack of unpaid invoices, someone found a thin journal, kept by date.

The first entries were nothing. Two logs short this week, and the Aldgrens gone north — fine, one less mouth to argue about plank thickness. A saw blade that needed resetting. Nineteen households. Sixty-one residents. Ordinary math, kept by someone with no idea yet that it would ever need keeping.

Then it started slipping. A boy came back from past the third stone not remembering two hours of it, checking his own hands like he expected them different. The bridge watch doubled — not because anyone thought it would help, the entry admitted flatly, but because it let people believe someone was doing something. Seventeen households. Fifteen. Eleven, and six people setting out for Riften at first light because someone had to try, whatever the miller thought of the odds.

Then the hand changed. A different, unsteadier writer picked up the same book: She didn't come back. Gwilin didn't either. Four did. I don't know why I'm writing in this. Somebody's got to keep it going, I suppose.

Six households left. Eighteen residents. A girl's boot found under her own bed with nothing else of her in it — the entry couldn't bring itself to cross her name off the list, and didn't.

Three households. Ten residents. Two of what's left are children too small for the road, one too old for it. The ale is gone. There was no entry after that one.

Somewhere in the house, a door swung shut on its own, the latch catching a full second after the door had already closed.

PART II: WHAT CAME FOR TEMBA
Movement caught their eye at a stable built into the back of the town's inn — a young woman leaning half out through the barn door, waving both arms before they were close enough to hear her. She was shouting for them to hurry, to bring everyone, the horses, the cart, all of it, into the stable now.

Someone reached for a spell to read her before trusting her. Nothing came back. No magic, no threat, nothing to find. And in the half-second it took to confirm that, she saw something behind them that they hadn't yet turned to look at. She gasped, and slammed the stable door shut in their faces rather than wait for them.

She had not been luring them. She had been trying to get them inside before it arrived, and she had run out of time.

Five of them came out of nowhere — bipedal, hooded, three short claws to a limb, three horns crowding a beaked snout. Whatever they were, they moved together, already grouped tight before anyone had finished drawing a weapon. Ismara was already positioned for it. She'd never rejoined the others after splitting off to search on her own, and from wherever that had put her, she was already behind them when the five showed themselves, and got the first hit in before any of them knew there was a fight starting. Initiative went out ragged after that: the world itself stuttered on the first roll, someone's whole first turn simply not arriving, spent instead, and then the fight was properly on.

