The last three died to ice and lightning together, and as they went, their bodies came apart from whatever shape had been forced onto them. For one terrible, precise second each one wore a person's shape again — a face, a stance, someone's particular way of standing — and then a fox, then a wolf, then a beaver, a troll, an elf, a man, spinning through every shape a thing could wear before settling, at the end, on none of them. A puddle. Nothing. They did not leave corpses. They left nothing at all.

Someone went to finish the last of them at close range with a drawn blade. The strike never landed. It skated off armor that simple steel had no purchase on at all. Whatever these creatures had been touched by, an edge alone wasn't going to be the thing that answered it.

They gathered what proof the ground would give them — the same three-fingered hand on every remaining set of claws, confirmation, not conjecture, that these had been people first.

PART III: THE INN THAT IS NOT A HAVEN
The woman from the stable let them in once the fight was done, no longer afraid to open the door. Inside, the stable was close and warm and overfull — three other horses already crowded into stalls built for two, a second brace nailed across the doors above the first, hay kicked into every corner. She led them through a low door at the back, down a short dark passage, and out into the common room, and barred the door behind them the instant they'd cleared it.

Ten people were sheltering there, none of them fit to climb anything. Two children asleep under a table. An old woman by the fire. A man with one arm splinted to his chest. Every window was shuttered and barred, one brace raw new wood where an older one had failed — and through the gap in that shutter, the outside face of it was raked with the same pale scoring they'd find later at the wreck. It was the middle of the afternoon, and the door was barred anyway.

A big man came off his feet before the bar had even fully dropped, one hand near an axe kept close under the counter of what was plainly his own bar, his own inn. He looked them over, decided they were more frightened arrivals until they made it plain they weren't, and only then actually looked at them.

He didn't explain unprompted. Asked, he told it in pieces, out of order, one hand flat on the bar like it was the only thing keeping him upright: the escort for Riften, second bend past the mill, something huge breathing right next to his ear before he ever saw it standing in the road. More teeth than its mouth had room for. It hadn't run at them. It had walked. Four came back.

Pressed on the fifth name specifically, he gave them only two words. She didn't. He wouldn't describe it twice.

Asked whether daylight bought them any safety at all, he was plain about it: it didn't. Whatever crossed here didn't care what the sun was doing — that was what had taken the Riften party, in the open, in daylight, gone before anyone could do anything about it. That was why the doors stayed shut even now, at two in the afternoon, over a fire with children asleep under the table. The nights were a different danger entirely — organized, purposeful, men coming through the whole town with torches and a chant, not something that had simply crossed over on its own. The men who might have stood against that were already dead. He was what was left.

He didn't ask them to fight for the town. He asked for something else, and it cost him visibly to say it. There was only one thing left that could actually help Ivarstead now — the Greybeards, coming down from the mountain. He needed them to ask. To beg, if it came to that. He couldn't leave these people, and he couldn't do anything more for them than he already had. They were his only remaining chance to save them.

He gave them a pack — Klimmek's, the supply run that had kept the monastery fed every week for years, and hadn't gone up this time. Whoever carried it up would be recognized for it, known, even if they weren't known themselves.

The old Blade in the back of the cart finally spoke up, unprompted, once the innkeeper had said his piece. He knew a little of what these things were on sight — not summoned, no chant or circle behind them, just a barrier thin enough in this one place to let something through on its own. He'd seen a city fall exactly this way once, two hundred years gone. This was that same door, starting to open. He didn't think the inn was safe to hold, and he said so plainly: bring everyone. Don't leave anyone behind at the foot of this.

It was Orion who said they should heal each other before they left — spend something on the people still in that inn before walking away from them. Coming from him, it was strange enough to say so out loud: shocking, hearing that out of him. Someone else said no anyway — didn't want to heal anyone, didn't see the point, would rather the sick stayed sick than spend anything on people they were about to leave behind. The offer went nowhere. They left the fire the way they'd found it.

Alfonso went instead to the old woman by the hearth, and knelt, and pressed something into her that read, to everyone else in the room, as a blessing — a quiet word, a hand laid gentle, the shape of comfort offered to someone already dying of being old and sick in a town that was dying with her. She thanked him for it. What actually passed into her, unseen by anyone at that fire, was not mercy. It was his own nature, exerted quietly into a body too frail to carry it. It made her worse, not better, and nobody there — not her, not the rest of the company — had any way of knowing it.

