PART IV: DUET
Alfonso reached into Davinia's held silence the way only he could — passed hand to hand until the rapier sat in Ismara's own grip, and coming awake fully Wakeful inside it from there.

He told her plainly what he'd found: a real counter to what had been done to her, a weapon against the hold Ismara kept on her body. He hadn't yet found the way to finish it — to expel her the rest of the way and give Davinia back whole. Until he did, he offered what he could: quieter, kinder memories laid gently over the worst of her own, to keep her from breaking against them while she waited.

Ismara didn't fight him for the ground. She turned it into a contest instead, memory against memory, a straight test of Magic between them — and won it outright, fourteen points clear. What she showed him for the win was Davinia's real weight: a training match at thirteen where she'd broken a boy's spine rather than lose it, praised for the same cruelty that had never once felt like a choice to her. Not the crippled boy alone. A carriage ride afterward, coin quietly paid to smooth it over, played to Davinia as her mother's tenderness rather than her mother's damage control. A senator's son she'd been meant to marry, and the broken nose she'd given him instead of the wedding. And under all of it, a fact Alfonso hadn't had before: this body, before Ismara ever took it, had carried a woman monstrously above the party's own weight — level twenty-five, against a company sitting somewhere around the ninth.

Losing the contest took the clean truth off the table. Alfonso had come in meaning to give her something honest. What he left her with instead, once Ismara had shown him exactly how outmatched he was, was a comforting lie in its place — kinder than what he actually knew now, and worse for being false. Ismara rose out of it like mist. We'll take it from here, she said, and meant it as more than a farewell.

