PART V: THE SECOND BEND
Before they left, the town's last able man told them where to find what remained of the Riften party — the second bend past the mill, west along the river. They went, and searched the wreck of the cart, and confirmed there what the fight in town had already shown them: the same creatures, the same claws, the same three fingers. No bodies. Whatever had happened to Temba's party, it had left nothing behind to bury — the same absence, doubled.

They took what could still be used. A hundred gold, scattered with the wreckage. Fifty steel arrows. A handful of steel swords that were worth almost nothing against what actually lived on this mountain now — one of them was melted down on the spot and reforged into something closer to a blade that might still bite, ice worked cold and sharp where fire had failed. The salvaged rope and pitons went to work on the cart itself, rigged into an anchor line against the wind they hadn't reached yet.

As they searched, more than one of them felt it plainly: something was watching from above, from higher up the mountain, patient and unhurried about it. Nothing came down for them. It didn't need to yet.

They gathered the company — every soul who traveled with them, not only the three who carried weapons for a living — and started back through the town one last time. One of their number stopped before the gate to leave the survivors with something to hold onto, and meant it more plainly than anything else said that day: Until death, all defeat exists in the mind.

The people left in that inn had the faces of everyone who has watched people they knew die across a handful of days. They said thank you, because there was nothing else left to say, and went back inside to wait.

The company crossed the bridge, reached the foot of the seven thousand steps, and began to climb.
